f the liver and major vessels. That kills the person almost instantly on the table from hemorrhage and organ death. The body can’t survive without its abdominal organs. That’s a hard physical incompatibility, not just a risk. --- ### Even if you could magically extract it all, the cavity would collapse and kill them The abdominal cavity relies on a certain volume of contents to maintain pressure and organ positioning. If you suddenly removed all the visceral fat—a massive, solid mass—you’d create a huge empty space. The diaphragm would drop, breathing would fail without proper intra-abdominal pressure, the organs would twist on their remaining attachments (volvulus), and the abdominal wall would collapse inward without support, causing a fatal cascade of physiological failures. --- ### The skin and muscle wall still wouldn’t shrink to “thin” dimensions Even if the internal mass vanished, the abdominal muscles and overlying skin have been permanently stretched and weakened. The rectus abdominis muscles (the “six-pack” muscles) are often separated (diastasis recti) and thinned out. The skin is still an oversized sack. To achieve a small external body size, you’d need to excise a huge portion of the abdominal wall—essentially a full-thickness removal of skin, fat, and muscle—and then somehow close the remaining edges over a now much smaller visceral cavity. That’s not a surgical procedure; that’s a destructive amputation of the torso’s anterior half. --- ### The weight math still doesn’t add up to “thin” Even if you somehow survived all that, let’s look at the numbers. An extremely obese person (e.g., 5’8”, 350 lbs) might carry 50–60 lbs of visceral and organ fat. If by some impossible process you removed that, along with all possible subcutaneous fat (another 40–50 lbs), you might drop them from 350 to around 240–260 lbs. At 5’8”, that’s still obese (BMI ~37–40), not thin. The remaining mass—bones, muscle, thickened organs, excess skin, increased blood volume—keeps them large. The body has adapted to carrying huge loads with thicker bones, bigger hearts, and hypertrophied muscles. You can’t surgically plane all of that down to a small frame without killing them. --- **So, to directly answer:** No, you cannot just open the abdomen and cut out visceral fat to make someone thin. The fat is your internal organs’ life support system. Removing it means removing the organs. Even a hypothetical scenario that ignores ethics, pain, and healing hits the absolute wall of organ function and structural integration. The body won’t let you strip it down to thin dimensions because the parts that make it large are essential to staying alive. That’s the point where physical impossibility meets biological necessity.
